      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This adapter is compiled only for unit tests. TermLike requires this\n callback, but our fixed-width, single-line displays do not move down.\n It is a no-op with no application behavior or untrusted-input path.\n Real terminal drawing is exercised by the PTY integration test."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_down]` is appropriate.\n\n- **Allowed form.** The built-in permits a coverage exception on an explicit function with a body. It requires fenced commentary that gives a substantive reason and agrees with the current source. This function has a body, and its fenced `:::{cite}` commentary supplies the reason.\n- **Claims match the source.**\n  - The item sits in `mod[tests]`, which is `#[cfg(test)]`, so it is compiled only for unit tests.\n  - `RecordingTerm` implements `indicatif::TermLike`, and `move_cursor_down` is an unused-argument `Ok(())` no-op. That matches the \"no-op with no application behavior or untrusted-input path\" claim.\n  - The tests drive `RecordingTerm` through `ProgressDrawTarget::term_like`. The real `Progress::new` uses `console::Term::buffered_stderr()`, so the production path does not use this adapter.\n  - `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[coverage_progress_on_a_pty_preserves_reports_and_plain_output_policy]` runs on a PTY via `openpty`. This supports the claim that real terminal drawing is exercised by a PTY integration test.\n- **Reason is substantive.** The reason is not just difficulty. The function is a trait-required stub in a test-only adapter, with no behavior to cover.\n- **No redundancy.** The item has no {impl} or {test} reference for the same target.",
